# Scheduled jobs — supercronic, container TZ=America/New_York, so every time
# below is ET and matches the times the command files state directly.
#
# The minutes are deliberately off :00/:15/:30. Every scheduler on the planet
# fires on those marks; :17 and :22 sidestep the herd at both the Schwab and
# Anthropic APIs for no cost.
#
# Labor Day 2026-09-07 is NOT special-cased. /deep-research §A and
# get_market_hours no-op it, at the cost of one wasted run. That is deliberate:
# a date hard-coded into a schedule file is a date that silently stops matching
# its rule — the exact defect commit 8f2171c removed from the dispatch prompt.
#
# Every job goes through scheduled-run.sh, which owns locking, the ET window
# guard, logging and the heartbeat. Nothing calls `claude` directly.

# --- research ------------------------------------------------------------
17 8  * * 1-5   /app/scripts/scheduled-run.sh preopen
22 16 * * 1-5   /app/scripts/scheduled-run.sh postclose
40 7  * * 6     /app/scripts/scheduled-run.sh weekly-universe

# --- monitoring (tick.md §F) ---------------------------------------------
# Every 15 minutes through the session, on the same off-the-herd minutes as
# everything else: :02 :17 :32 :47. The 09:02 and 09:17 firings are skipped by
# the job's own 09:30-16:00 ET window guard rather than by a narrower cron
# expression — the guard is the single source of truth for when a job may run,
# and encoding the same boundary twice is how the two drift apart. Last sweep
# of the day is 15:47; the 16:22 postclose owns everything after the bell.
#
# This is DETECTION ONLY. The sweep has no order tools, so a tripped watch is
# relayed to Discord for a human to act on — it is not the live §E loop.
2-59/15 9-15 * * 1-5   /app/scripts/scheduled-run.sh tick

# --- session close (§7.2) -------------------------------------------------
# 16:04 ET, four minutes after the bell so the marks are closing marks, and
# eighteen before the postclose deep run that reads the file it writes.
#
# This job's ABSENCE was a live defect until 2026-08-26. tick.md §B5 resolves
# the high-water mark from the most recent status/ file; with nothing writing
# one, the mark froze and the orphan-ledger recovery rule stood ready to write
# ALERT.md — closing-only, no buys — on the morning after any day the account
# made a new high. It self-halted on wins and only on wins. 2026-08-26 cleared
# the threshold by $7.36.
#
# Ordering against postclose is a real constraint, not a preference: the job's
# 720s timeout ends the worst case at 16:16, six minutes clear of 16:22.
4  16 * * 1-5   /app/scripts/scheduled-run.sh sessionclose

# --- execution (playbook §7) ---------------------------------------------
# The ONLY job with order tools. Offset five minutes behind the tick so it acts
# on a book state that was just swept rather than one it re-derives.
#
# Its 1800s budget deliberately exceeds this 15-minute cadence: an entry needs
# two Discord approvals (the entry, then its mandatory §3.4 stop) at up to 600s
# each, plus the fill poll between them. Overlapping firings find the lock held
# and exit — while an order is in flight a second executor is the last thing
# anyone wants, and that is the concurrency control, not an accident of timing.
#
# Every order still blocks on Chris's ✅/❌ in #llm-yolo. Nothing here is
# unapproved; it is unattended, which CLAUDE.md §0 distinguishes explicitly.
7-59/15 9-15 * * 1-5   /app/scripts/scheduled-run.sh execute

# --- watchdogs (Phase 3) --------------------------------------------------
5  7  * * *     /app/scripts/token-watchdog.sh
35 9  * * 1-5   /app/scripts/job-deadman.sh

# --- deployment (Phase 1b) ------------------------------------------------
# deploy.sh is deliberately NOT here. It runs from the HOST's crontab
# (bootstrap-server.sh installs it), for two reasons that are both structural:
# this container has no docker CLI and no socket, so the job could only ever
# exit 2 — a deploy schedule that silently never deploys; and a deployer that
# lives inside the thing it deploys kills itself the moment compose recreates
# its own container, dying before the health check and rollback it exists to
# perform. check-consistency.sh check 8 enforces this stays true.
